#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e75e05b-69b7-5d89-ab44-68e13e00bf09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287620} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb4c02c-f63c-5f7c-9612-95116e8b7a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287621} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbed97b-6ddd-57c8-b99e-90a38430c8d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287622} {:answer/id #uuid "a5353d77-4708-5328-a5b2-22e0a3c0f7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287623} {:answer/id #uuid "d8310729-e44c-5cf3-bcde-0a277495a68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5353d77-4708-5328-a5b2-22e0a3c0f7a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287623} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ff4300-92d1-53b6-9ddf-f92ba758026a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1745afc2-b971-5788-afd0-97c27ea03061" :question/math-academy-id "q-120893" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62e3915-c58c-55f8-83f1-1e44a85809d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,410" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627} {:answer/id #uuid "45b82809-5996-5b21-b3e5-9bec5e7610fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287628} {:answer/id #uuid "08887998-3606-56a2-891d-bfd9a1446b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287629} {:answer/id #uuid "c88bde2b-87da-50ec-9443-583f1c383eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287630} {:answer/id #uuid "eea58201-2105-5006-a837-ca10eaf2abb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62e3915-c58c-55f8-83f1-1e44a85809d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,410" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627} :answer-field/id #uuid "04d207a6-7757-5b40-880c-1b92a1792a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d74b0693-7ccd-5249-bfd3-82bffd3a3cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120982" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.4$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.4)=66.421{…}^{∘}≈{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${66.42}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/9d2b6dbe16a9537db672ddb3b369dbb49132a9f8860c774f3b8c79d478711f95.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{66.42}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=66.42\\cdot (-66.42)≈-4\\,410\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0019a227-6971-576a-a3f9-e0817a13c2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287634} {:answer/id #uuid "2af34539-bd76-53e3-a552-6716d7a54801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287635} {:answer/id #uuid "2000c653-cef2-55b5-b9f0-f9945ea3fd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘},\\,{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287636} {:answer/id #uuid "34ac7b21-1551-5fc0-ad7c-46509179f2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287637} {:answer/id #uuid "75c2803e-48a1-5ae5-b05e-b1b5b5a763d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34ac7b21-1551-5fc0-ad7c-46509179f2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287637} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58f761f-635d-538b-8f10-50674122b23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65cd22dc-7a3f-567a-99e3-fbc144596450" :question/math-academy-id "q-121005" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26666440-d376-5cb6-b922-9850e5c42eb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aed24c60-6961-5ff5-9a3f-7add6d0f2e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aed24c60-6961-5ff5-9a3f-7add6d0f2e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eadacaa-8b63-5d1a-8319-eecb56549b1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1531d05-7142-545c-a3a8-63fc8ecb5b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1531d05-7142-545c-a3a8-63fc8ecb5b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287643} :answer-field/id #uuid "dab69fb3-fbc8-5cf3-a29d-bbe0f31c7b07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e300674-c74b-5fe9-8494-1fd913cb8b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-274587" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.7$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.7)=(45.572\\,9…)^{∘}≈{45.573}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45.573}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={46}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/3ff8bb94afda0843fa6f22802ae70ca98d7e6856e8be2911ab1cb3ea2cccd2c1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{46}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-46}^{∘}$ and $x={46}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efc34ef6-2573-50ca-bd34-43468d41ef43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efc34ef6-2573-50ca-bd34-43468d41ef43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287646} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a94393f-ba4b-5b59-8b2b-c3e6e7cdbfcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39aae2c6-791e-52d0-9e6a-2023a4352a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-338741" :question/problem "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9=5$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nHowever, since the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}]}
